#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091934 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece25ba-5df0-5b73-8cc5-7788b17fdcc4" :knowledge-point/title "The Joint PDF of a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "48d7b03b-c991-574a-bf15-8765ca43724b" :question/math-academy-id "e-11113" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of $X_1$ and $X_2$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$\\sigma_1^2=9,\\qquad \\sigma_2^2 = 1, \\qquad \\rho = \\dfrac1{3}$.\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n$f(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac1k\\,\\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)$,\n\nwhere $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ is the mean vector and $\\Sigma$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T\n$$\n\nis a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$, given by\n\n$$\nμ = (μ_{1}; μ_{2}), Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & σ_{12} \\\\ σ_{21} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & ρσ_{1}σ_{2} \\\\ ρσ_{1}σ_{2} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2_i = \\textrm{Var}[X_i]\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sigma_{12} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2]\n$$\n\nand $\\rho$ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{\\mid \\Sigma \\mid}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left(-\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nand $\\mid \\Sigma \\mid$ denotes the determinant of $\\Sigma$.\n\nUsing the given values of $\\sigma^2_1, \\sigma^2_2$, and $\\rho$, our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{9} \\cdot \\sqrt{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{9} \\cdot \\sqrt{1} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mid \\Sigma \\mid = 9\\cdot 1 - 1\\cdot 1 = 8\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{1}{2π\\sqrt{8}}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(x - μ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4 π 2}\\exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x - μ)^{T}Σ^{-1}(x - μ))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nk=4\\pi\\sqrt 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091935 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092331 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c646fe15-1709-599e-b5d5-308b1dcb3271" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "90ff4bf9-f8d3-5179-9d02-0e0a46a3cc98" :question/math-academy-id "e-11114" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1,X_2]^T$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ given by\n$μ = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the distribution of $Y = 3X_1 + X_2$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T\n$$\n\nis a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$, given by\n\n$$\nμ = \\begin{bmatrix}μ_{1} \\\\ μ_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}σ_{1}^{2} & σ_{12} \\\\ σ_{21} & σ_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y$, defined as\n\n$$\nY= aX_1 + bX_2\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= aμ_{1} + bμ_{2}, \\\\\nVar[Y] &= a^{2}σ_{1}^{2} + b^{2}σ_{2}^{2} + 2abσ_{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\mu_1 = 2$ and $\\mu_2 = -1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= E[3X_{1} + X_{2}] \\\\\n&= 3E[X_{1}] + E[X_{2}] \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot μ_{1} + μ_{2} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2 + (-1) \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1^2 = 4, \\sigma_2^2 = 7\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\sigma_{12} = \\sigma_{21} = -3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[Y] &= Var[3X_{1} + X_{2}] \\\\\n&= 3^{2} \\cdot 4 + 1^{2} \\cdot 7 + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-3) \\\\\n&= 36 + 7 - 18 \\\\\n&= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$Y \\sim N(5,25)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091936 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092332 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2025f8-a9cf-5e30-bb03-4909aefdd7e2" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Multivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "f9e6c722-6017-57b9-9de6-c83c2f14c8c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-11115" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2,X_3]^T$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ given by\n$μ = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind the distribution of $Y = X_1 -2X_2 +4 X_3$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k]^T\n$$\n\nis a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$, then the random variable $Y$, defined as\n\n$$\nY= \\mathbf c^T \\mathbf X = \\sum_{i=1}^k c_i X_i\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $\\mathbf c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= c^{T}μ, \\\\\nVar[Y] &= c^{T}Σc\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of\n\n$$\nY = X_1 -2X_2 +4 X_3\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= c^{T}μ \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 1 - 4 + 4 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[Y] &= c^{T}Σc \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= 12 - 6 + 56 \\\\\n&= 62\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY\\sim N(1,62)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092331 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c646fe15-1709-599e-b5d5-308b1dcb3271" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a911939-3a0e-5aac-b17e-fdc58da67812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(-2, \\sqrt{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089532} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e5fb70-5050-5e19-957e-96c289684bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(-2, \\sqrt{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089533} {:answer/id #uuid "15f1e939-abbd-5b53-aa20-c3d0077860bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(-2, 13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089534} {:answer/id #uuid "36411804-3b00-59ed-9e6f-d497bb4f7ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(-2, 17)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089535} {:answer/id #uuid "3c2d6cd8-5072-50fd-9c93-969fb8fa023f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(-2, \\sqrt{17})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089536}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e35154dd-0662-5424-962f-17ec732aca8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089531}] :question/id #uuid "695d4d94-877b-5df0-9f8f-05a7c0cd7954" :question/math-academy-id "q-143542" :question/problem "Let $X = [X_{1}, X_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $μ$ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n$μ = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the distribution of $Y = X_{1} - 2X_{2}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece25ba-5df0-5b73-8cc5-7788b17fdcc4" :knowledge-point/title "The Joint PDF of a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be6f80eb-a499-50b3-af2e-b81ab0a4bb2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089646} {:answer/id #uuid "0ddb1276-fbbf-51ec-994f-c687bbc7a938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089647} {:answer/id #uuid "376611a2-b476-58a2-b914-73ce0fcbcbfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089648} {:answer/id #uuid "5abcbb3e-f009-59dc-9ec4-9275eb820be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089649} {:answer/id #uuid "63ad13e1-e3c8-527f-9ff8-fd44b3b035ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089650}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e306f3ba-57fb-5958-a190-c31ffdd43476" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089645}] :question/id #uuid "0707e3bd-3da9-5662-809b-216f2be6b97a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159689" :question/problem "Let $X = [X_{1}, X_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of $X_{1}$ 